        "text": "while making their rounds, bees cross-pollinate plants \n \nThe East African Rift System makes up the western and eastern continental rifts stretching from the \nAfar region of Ethiopia down to Mozambique.  It is an active continental rift that began millions of years ago, \nsplitting at 7 mm annually. There are three active volcanoes in the continent: the Ol  Doinyo Lengai in \nTanzania, and Dalla  Filla and Erta Ale in Ethiopia. And the Erta Ale is one of the world’s most active \nvolcanoes and possibly the longest -existing lava lakes in the world. The regular eruption of these volcanoes \nalong the rift and new insights into the break -up of the continents add to the belief that the continent may be \nsplitting to form a new ocean. \nVictoria microplate, one of the largest continental microplates, is a rift branch which, along chains of \ndeep lakes and volcanoes, makes up the several features of the East African Rift System. Located between the \neastern and western branches of the Rift, the Victoria microplate was found to be rotating counter -clockwise \nfor the last two years with respect to the African plate, the major tectonic plate constituting most of the African \ncontinent. It was found to rotate in the opposite direction to all other neighbouring microplates in the region. \nWhile this gives researchers new insights into the splitting process of East African Rift System, the “Y” -\nshaped-end of the rift at the Afar region is getting more attention, as to where an ocean will likely be forme d \nif the splits continue. The “Y”-shaped junction is where the African, Somalian, Arabian tectonic plates meet \nnear Djibouti and Eritrea and it is associated with active volcanoes including the Erta Ale volcano. \nResearchers believe that volcanic activity in the region suggests a rift-to-ridge transition. The Erta Ale \nhas been erupting constantly for over 50 years and it is believed that as Erta Ale continue to erupt, a new \nnarrow ocean basin with its mid -ocean ridge will be formed. Nevertheless, researchers are uncertain about \nwhether the split will continue and an ocean will be formed. ...., it will take tens of millions of years for an \nocean to be eventually formed.    \n \n \n \nLiterasi dalam Bahasa Inggris\n: 20\n: 20 menit\nPROSUS INTEN \nT.A 2025/2026"
