    "question": "Certain traits are embedded in hard selling. Show such trait \nThere is more than one correct answer.  \nClick on every correct answer!   \n☐ Prioritizing the immediacy of the deal   \n☐ Giving the customers enough time to review an offer  \n☐ Aiming at closing the deal as quickly as possible \n☐ Being irrelevant for procrastinator \n☐ Giving commission to those who sell the product quickly \n \n \n \n \n \nLiterasi dalam Bahasa Inggris\n: 20\n: 20 menit\nPROSUS INTEN \nT.A 2025/2026\n \nThe television debates between Da Silva  and Sly Costa  became a tremendously national issue \nparticularly after they were analyzed insightfully by an expert in politics, Andrew Chang, who wrote a best-\nselling book analyzing the issue. He contended that the debates had to be analy zed within the context of the \nexplosion in the field of communication. During the previous decades, citizens had purchased television sets \nat a phenomenal rate. By the evening of the debate, 88 percent of the country’s families owned a television \nset, and a very large percentage tuned into the debate. \nThe format was less like a debate than like a press conference. Each candidate was allowed an opening \nstatement of eight minutes, and then two and a half minutes to respond to each question proposed by a panel. \nThere was no provision for dialogue between t he candidates. As Chang observed, despite this format, Sly \nCosta proceeded as though he were in a personal debate with Da Silva, trying to score points from the reporters \non the panel. In contrast, Da Silva spoke directly to the television viewers, concentrating on creating a dynamic \nand appealing image to influence them.  \nLater, Da Silva claimed that the 2024’s debates were the single most important factor in the election. \nIn Chang’s view, the debates really changed the direction of the campaign. From the 2024’s research studies, \nincluding the Gallup Poll, it appeared that Da Silva had gained at least 2 million votes  as a result of the \ntelevised programs. When you consider that Da Silva won by a little more than 100,000 votes, the debates \nsurely made the difference. It has been clear to candidates since then that television debates are a very powerful \ntool.",
