        "text": "The impact of caffeine on the foetal brain is uncertain, \nwhether it is varied from one trimester to the next or whether \nthese structural reforms depend on the stage of pregnancy.  \n \nTEXT 1 \n \nRecently, there has been a slow uphill battle for the fashion industry to make more sustainable  \nclothing from recycled materials like plastic bottles or even from recycled clothing and  clothing scraps. \nWhile this is a good thing, costs are still relatively high, and not many people will want to spend $100+ on \ntwo articles of clothing just because it’s environmentally friendly. A          better alternative would be buying \nclothing second-hand. What does second -hand mean? Well, it’s  synonymous with thrift store shopping. \nThat being said, there are a plethora of reasons why thrifting  is better than fast fashion. It is good for the \nenvironment and the economy.      \nThrifting is a great way to get the most out of your buck. Some thrift stores even offer   discounts. \nYou can even “upcycle” clothing. Upcycling here means taking a piece of clothing    and making it more to \nyour liking or more fashionable, which allows you to save money.              \nNow the question at hand is “why is buying second -hand good for the environment?” First of all, \nthrifting   is good for the environment because it reduces the amount of plastic that ends up at the dump. \nAlso, thrifting reduces the number of wasted resources that get burnt up from making new clothing or other         \ntextile products. If you looked it up, you would find that there is an astronomical amount of water  used in \ntextile production that is incredibly wasteful. Specifically, clothing/textile product ion requires around 9.3 \nbillion tons of water annually. Besides, the fashion industry is calculated to be responsible for about 10 % \nof global carbon emissions. However, thrifting helps to cut pollution and carbon emissions. \nLet’s address the concerns you might have with buying second -hand clothes. The biggest concern \nfor most people is cleanliness. Generally, thrift stores are sanitary and have policies about those matters. \nBesides, the majority of customers will usually immediately wash  clothing before wearing them. Those \nthings can help people in selecting clothes in thrift stores. Thrift stores also sort through clothing   to make \nsure that they aren’t damaged, dirty, stained, or don’t smell bad.                                   \nSecond-hand shopping isn’t only good for your bank account. It’s also great for the environment.   \nLast but not least is this: Second-hand shopping isn’t only done in-store. If you don’t like shopping in person, \nthere are hundreds of online thrifting options right at your fingertips. \n \n \n \nLiterasi dalam Bahasa Inggris\n: 20\n: 20 menit\nPROSUS INTEN \nT.A 2025/2026"
